# Z80 breadboard computer — the PainfulDiodes design (USB-FIFO variant).
#
# The bench is the deliverable. This is the SECOND Z80 in the catalog and it
# does not replace the first: `z80-bench` answers "how do I talk to a modern
# computer?" with an MC6850 ACIA; this one answers it with an FTDI UM245R
# parallel USB FIFO. Same question, two real answers — which is the point.
#
# THE MACHINE, as bw-board's extractZ80Machine reads it off the wiring:
#
#   MAP ROM $0000-$7FFF     /ROM_CE = /MREQ OR A15   (memory request, A15 low)
#   MAP RAM $8000-$FFFF     /RAM_CE = /MREQ OR /A15  (memory request, A15 high)
#
# There is no CHIP line, and its absence is honest: the machine's only I/O
# device is a UM245R USB FIFO, and no engine part models one yet. The decode
# that would drive it IS on the board and IS wired — glue2/glue3/glue4 produce
# /RD_PORT_0, /RD_PORT_1 and WR_PORT_1 exactly as the original does — the
# strobes simply end at unconnected gate outputs. See EXPECTED.md.
#
# The port decode, for the record (A0 alone selects; A0 is ignored on writes):
#
#   /RD_PORT_0 = A0  OR (/RD OR /IORQ)   IN from an even port  -> status byte
#   /RD_PORT_1 = /A0 OR (/RD OR /IORQ)   IN from an odd port   -> FIFO read
#   /WR_PORT_1 = /IORQ OR /WR            OUT to any port       -> FIFO write
#
# The Z80 program axis HAS landed, so this file gains the real program the
# older comment here promised. sb3Creator's Z80 core emits `sdcc -mz80` C for
# exactly the silicon this board carries: `z80Hw()` maps `OUT0-7` and `IN0-7`
# onto a 74HC374 write latch and a 74HC244 read buffer at I/O port 0, and
# bw-board's extractZ80Machine reads that same pair off this wiring as
# `latch1` and `in1`, both `at: 0`. The emitter and the extractor agree about
# the machine because they are describing the same board.
#
# The walking light below is the first program of every Searle-lineage build.
# The LEDs it drives are led0..led7 on the latch's Q pins.
#
# The board's INPUT half -- the DIP switches on the '244 -- is deliberately not
# declared. An earlier draft named it as `PIN sw0 = IN0 INPUT` to show the axis
# existed, and lite's declared-pins-wired gate was right to object: this program
# never reads it, so the declaration promised a wire it did not use. A pin a
# program does not read is not documentation, it is an unbacked claim.
#
# What is STILL absent is unchanged and still honest: the UM245R USB FIFO has
# no engine part, so /RD_PORT_0, /RD_PORT_1 and WR_PORT_1 remain wired to
# unconnected gate outputs. That is a different axis from the '374/'244 pair,
# and this program does not touch it.
DEVICE Z80
CLOCK 7372800
PIN l0 = OUT0 OUTPUT
PIN l1 = OUT1 OUTPUT
PIN l2 = OUT2 OUTPUT
PIN l3 = OUT3 OUTPUT
PIN l4 = OUT4 OUTPUT
PIN l5 = OUT5 OUTPUT
PIN l6 = OUT6 OUTPUT
PIN l7 = OUT7 OUTPUT

WHEN flag clicked:
  FOREVER:
    turn on l0
    wait 0.1 seconds
    turn off l0
    turn on l1
    wait 0.1 seconds
    turn off l1
    turn on l2
    wait 0.1 seconds
    turn off l2
    turn on l3
    wait 0.1 seconds
    turn off l3
    turn on l4
    wait 0.1 seconds
    turn off l4
    turn on l5
    wait 0.1 seconds
    turn off l5
    turn on l6
    wait 0.1 seconds
    turn off l6
    turn on l7
    wait 0.1 seconds
    turn off l7
